# Redirect and rewrite rules for https://meander-eoc.pages.dev.
#
# This file deliberately contains no rules. That is the decision, not an
# omission, and it is written down here because the obvious edit — adding a
# single-page-application catch-all — would break the site.
#
# ── why there is no `/* /index.html 200` ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# Two facts from Cloudflare's own documentation, and they compose badly:
#
#   "The order of your redirects matter. If there are multiple redirects for the
#    same source path, the top-most redirect is applied."
#   "Redirects are always followed, regardless of whether or not an asset
#    matches the incoming request."
#
# Rules are consulted BEFORE the asset lookup. A catch-all is therefore not a
# fallback for paths that have no file — it matches everything, first, and wins.
# `/* /index.html 200` would serve the HTML shell for /assets/index-*.js, for
# /sw.js, for every icon. The site would not render at all.
#
# And it is not needed. Pages already does exactly the SPA fallback we want,
# automatically, triggered by the absence of a top-level 404.html:
#
#   "If your project does not include a top-level 404.html file, Pages assumes
#    that you are deploying a single-page application. Pages' default
#    single-page application behavior matches all incoming paths to the root."
#
# Verified against the live site: /r/abc, /some/deep/link and /?s=xyz all return
# 200 with the shell, with no rules file present at all. Permalinks are query
# strings in any case — permalink.js:150 uses replaceState and never pushState,
# so a shared link is `/?from=…&to=…`, which is the root and always a real asset.
#
# So: do not add a 404.html to the build either. That single file would switch
# the SPA fallback off for every path at once.
#
# ── the /api/* hole, and why it is not closed here ────────────────────────────
#
# GET https://meander-eoc.pages.dev/api/health returns 200 with an HTML body
# today, because of that same automatic fallback. If the app ever made a
# same-origin API call it would get the shell instead of JSON, and client.js
# calls res.json() unguarded at :112, :160 and :215 — so the user would see a
# raw `Unexpected token '<'` SyntaxError in the error banner, and read aloud by
# the live region, with nothing pointing at the real cause.
#
# `_redirects` cannot fix this. Cloudflare supports 200 and the redirect codes
# 301, 302, 303, 307 and 308, and documents `/blog/* /blog/404.html 404` as an
# explicitly UNSUPPORTED example. There is no rule that answers 404, and a
# redirect to any other path lands back on the same fallback.
#
# It is closed at the other end instead, where the real defect is: the app only
# makes same-origin calls when VITE_API_BASE is unset at build time, and
# vite.config.js now fails the production build when it is. A misconfigured
# deploy stops before it ships rather than producing an app that talks to
# itself. See client.js:19 for why empty means same-origin, and DEPLOY.md's
# "things that will look like bugs" table for the symptom.
#
# If a real 404 on /api/* is ever wanted, it needs a Pages Function
# (functions/api/[[path]].js) — a Worker, and a runtime dependency this project
# does not currently have.
